      <Patch Id="SJKJnEcOChzOtJqs8z6FA7">
        <Canvas Id="QZPAqNRvnNIOwWBogWacV8" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="KRkO9YjrT9iPe5onSIFN1j" Bounds="129,53,571,297" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="How to build your own transition type.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A transition decides how a public channel moves to a new value. TriggerPreset and GoToValue both take one on their Transition input. Bezier and Tween ship with vvvv. Here we build a third one based on the DeNiro node, which behaves like a taxi driver: it accelerates, drives with a constant speed and decelerates.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Three parts are needed:&#xD;&#xA;1. A model (ITransitionModel) that holds the parameters of the transition. We expose the main pins of DeNiro: Acceleration, Max Velocity, Cyclic and Constant Drive.&#xD;&#xA;2. A transition (ITransition) that does the actual work while it runs.&#xD;&#xA;3. A node that produces the model. This is what you place in a patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Click A and B and watch the Position channel. Note that the duration only has to be greater than 0 to start a transition at all: how long it really takes depends on the parameters of DeNiro.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
